const body = `<h1>6.4c &#8212; Card Deck: Array of Objects</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Expand Application. You've built arrays of primitives
    &#8212; now fill an array with your own Card objects. Shuffle, deal, and
    print. Every card game starts here.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Build a <code>Deck</code> class that creates 52 Card objects, stores
    them in an array, shuffles them, and deals 5 cards.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">=== DECK (first 5 after shuffle) ===
7 of Hearts
King of Diamonds
3 of Spades
Ace of Clubs
10 of Hearts</span></code></pre>

    <p><strong>Reference:</strong>
    <a href="../docs/unit-06/card-deck.html">Card Deck Walkthrough</a> &#8212;
    full reference.</p>

    <h3>Build It: Deck</h3>

    <p>Create <code>Deck.java</code>. You'll need your <code>Card.java</code>
    from 5.4b. Start with this skeleton:</p>

    <pre><code>public class Deck {
    private Card[] cards;
    private int nextCard;  // tracks which card to deal next

    public Deck() {
        cards = new Card[52];
        nextCard = 0;

        String[] suits = {"Spades", "Hearts", "Diamonds", "Clubs"};
        String[] ranks = {"Ace","2","3","4","5","6","7","8","9","10",
                          "Jack","Queen","King"};

        // YOUR CODE: nested loops to fill cards[]
        // Outer loop: suits. Inner loop: ranks.
        // Each iteration: cards[something] = new Card(suit, rank);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <p>You have two arrays (suits has 4, ranks has 13) and need
        to fill 52 slots. How do you compute the array index? If you
        use <code>cards[i * 13 + j]</code>, trace it: suit index 0
        rank index 0 goes to slot 0. Suit index 1 rank index 0 goes
        to slot 13. Check your math before coding.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Shuffle Method</h3>

    <p>Here's the shuffle algorithm. Type it yourself &#8212; don't copy-paste:</p>

    <pre><code>public void shuffle() {
    for (int i = 0; i < cards.length; i++) {
        int swapIndex = (int) (Math.random() * cards.length);
        Card temp = cards[i];
        cards[i] = cards[swapIndex];
        cards[swapIndex] = temp;
    }
    nextCard = 0;  // reset the deal position
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is the swap temporary variable <code>Card temp</code>
                and not <code>int temp</code>? What type must it be?</li>
            <li>What does setting <code>nextCard = 0</code> after shuffle
                do? What would happen if you left it unchanged?</li>
            <li>If you shuffled 52 cards, how many total swaps does
                this loop do? Is every swap productive?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Deal Method</h3>

    <pre><code>public Card deal() {
    if (nextCard < cards.length) {
        Card c = cards[nextCard];
        nextCard++;
        return c;
    }
    return null;  // deck is empty
}</code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Extend It (pick one)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Add a <code>dealHand(int n)</code> method that returns
                an array of n cards.</li>
            <li>Add a method that checks if a dealt hand contains a
                pair, three of a kind, or a flush.</li>
            <li>Write a <code>DeckTester</code> that deals two 5-card
                hands and prints both.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

